# THE GUARD REVISION THIS REPOSITORY AUDITED, BY CONTENT.
#
# .github/workflows/release.yml pins the shared release guards by commit
# SHA. A SHA is content-addressed and therefore immutable, so the pin
# cannot be edited under us. What a SHA does NOT do is tell a reader what
# is in it, and nothing states which BYTES were looked at before pinning
# them. That is what this file is for.
#
# Every file the guards execute is recorded below by SHA-256, taken at
# the pinned revision. tests/test_guard_pins.sh re-fetches each one and
# refuses any difference, so:
#
#   * bumping the pin without re-auditing goes RED, because the digests
#     below describe a revision the workflow no longer names;
#   * a pin that resolves to bytes other than the audited ones goes RED,
#     which is the falsifiable form of "we ran what we pinned";
#   * the check runs offline of any claim the guards make about
#     themselves, before a tag is ever pushed.
#
# WHAT REMAINS TRUSTED, stated plainly rather than papered over: that
# GitHub runs the workflow file living at the pinned SHA. A workflow
# cannot establish its own identity from inside itself, because a
# substituted file could simply print the expected answer. That is the
# platform's guarantee and the reason `uses:@<sha>` is written by SHA.
# Everything downstream of it is checked here by content.
#
# THE DIGESTS BELOW WERE WRITTEN BY `bash fleet/guard_pins.sh`, which
# fetched each file at 2db00705e05b1774cf41eb58006e6533cfca4d55 and hashed it. Do not
# hand-edit them, and do not copy this file from another repository: a
# copied record passes every check while having audited nothing, because
# the digests are genuinely the pinned revision's bytes. What a copy
# cannot carry is the reading.
#
# TO RE-AUDIT AFTER A PIN BUMP: read the diff, then re-run
# `bash fleet/guard_pins.sh <this repository>` and rewrite the note below.
#
#   https://github.com/nelsonduarte/capa-language/compare/<old>...2db00705e05b1774cf41eb58006e6533cfca4d55

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUDIT NOTE, 2db0070. FIRST PIN IN THIS REPOSITORY, so there is no
# previous revision and no diff to read; the audit is of the five files
# themselves. All five were fetched at this revision, digested
# independently of the list below (the five digests agreed), and read
# rather than accepted on those digests. This note records what reading
# them established about how this repository is wired.
#
# tools/capa_floor.sh (80 lines) mattered most here, because it SELECTS
# the binary the clean room proves the artefact against: it reads the
# `capa` key of the [package] table and the guard downloads that release.
# Run against this repository's manifest it prints 1.18.1, which is the
# floor measured in capa.toml and the first release whose binary can run
# `capa test` at all. Two properties were checked against this manifest
# rather than assumed. The read is scoped to [package] by an awk that
# resets on every `[`, so neither the several `capa = ">=..."` strings
# quoted inside this manifest's comment blocks nor the `[capabilities]`
# and `[dependencies.capa_hash]` tables below it can be picked up; and
# it refuses anything but an exact version or a plain `>=X.Y.Z`, which
# is why the floor is written that way and not as a range. Its own
# header says nothing in the compiler enforces the field. That stopped
# being true in 1.19.0, whose capa/pkg/_floor.py makes a ROOT manifest's
# floor a hard error and a DEPENDENCY's floor a warning, and the split
# is the reason capa_hash's own false floor does not bite this package.
# The two grammars agree on this manifest.
#
# tools/clean_room_build.sh (223 lines) runs the consumer flow this
# repository supplies, so its contract is the one release.yml's
# `consumer-commands` block has to satisfy. Three things read here
# shaped that block. Each command is executed as `bash -c "<line>"` with
# `set -e` off and the status read explicitly, so a line may be a
# pipeline: the single negative is written as
# `capa --check leaky_example.capa 2>&1 | grep -q "..."` and that is a
# supported shape rather than a hopeful one. It runs `unset CAPA_PATH`
# before the first command, so nothing can reach back into the
# development tree. And it refuses a room that is not empty, which is
# the isolation the eight entry points are compiled under, and the
# reason the closure measurement in capa.toml was repeated against an
# extracted tarball rather than only in place.
#
# tools/check_tag_version.sh (112 lines) compares the pushed tag with
# the [package] version by the same table-scoped awk. Run here against
# capa.toml with v0.2.0 it prints "OK: tag v0.2.0 matches capa.toml
# [package] version 0.2.0". It takes a third argument naming the table,
# which this caller does not pass, so the default [package] applies.
#
# tools/verify_guard_digests.sh (148 lines) is the script the five
# digests below cause to run, so omitting it from this record would be
# omitting a file this repository makes execute. It fails closed on a
# missing file, a malformed line, a path containing `..`, and on an
# EMPTY digest list, which is the case worth having: "verified nothing"
# must not render as "verified".
#
# .github/workflows/release-guards.yml (608 lines) is the caller of the
# other four. Read for what it does with our inputs rather than
# end-to-end line by line, and that is stated so the bound of this audit
# is visible: both guard jobs take `contents: read` and no id-token,
# both fail closed on an empty `job.workflow_sha` before any checkout,
# the compiler binary is downloaded from the release page for the
# version capa_floor.sh printed and its own SLSA provenance verified
# with `gh attestation verify` before it is put on PATH, `capa --version`
# is then required to report that same version, and guard 2 reconstructs
# the tarball from the tag with the same `git archive` invocation the
# publish job uses, exporting `tarball-sha256` so the caller can close
# the gap between what was verified and what is uploaded. release.yml
# closes it.
#
# WHY THIS REVISION AND NOT THE COMPILER'S CURRENT MAIN. Every other
# repository in this fleet pins 2db0070, and two of the five files have
# moved since: release-guards.yml by one line and capa_floor.sh by 21,
# tightening its version regex to exactly three numeric components. A
# bump is a fleet-wide exercise, because every adopter's record must be
# re-audited against the new bytes, and doing it in one repository would
# leave sixteen records describing a revision only sixteen of them run.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

